<GuideLayout
  title="Squat-Proof Testing Protocol for Activewear Leggings"
  type="QUALITY CONTROL"
  description="A buyer-friendly squat-proof testing protocol for activewear leggings covering opacity, fabric weight, color, stretch direction and sample approval."
>
  <p>Squat-proof performance is one of the most important quality signals for leggings. Buyers should not rely only on fabric weight or supplier promises. A clear test protocol helps protect brand reputation before bulk production.</p>

  <h2>Testing Steps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Check opacity in natural light and bright indoor light.</li>
    <li>Test dark, light and seasonal colors separately.</li>
    <li>Review opacity across size grading, especially larger sizes.</li>
    <li>Stretch the garment in real movement positions, not only by hand.</li>
    <li>Confirm results after washing and drying.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Color Matters</h2>
  <p>Light colors, heather shades and certain recycled yarn blends may show more transparency. The same fabric construction can perform differently across colorways.</p>

  <h2>Fitclo QC Note</h2>
  <p>Fitclo checks fabric opacity, GSM, stretch recovery and garment fit before bulk approval so brands can reduce the risk of transparent leggings reaching customers.</p>
</GuideLayout>
